venus 0123: a read only power + thermal probe of the PMIC and the
AppleSmartBattery service that publishes it

The battery of the venus board (Xiaomi Mi 11 / SM8350) has no fuel gauge
on the application processor side: the charger and the gauge live behind
the ADSP charger protection domain (logs/research/06-power-battery-charging.md
section 2.4).  What IS reachable over plain SPMI is the PM8350B itself:
the SMB5 charger registers and the BCL v5 block give the real battery
voltage (0x4776) and current (0x4786), the charge state, the error bits
and the power path, and the PMIC temperature alarm blocks (peripheral
0x0a) plus the PMK8350 VADC / ADC7 (peripheral 0x31) give the thermal
state (logs/research/26-thermal-plan.md, section 5.0).

This patch adds the missing half of that: a probe that samples those
registers and an IOKit service that publishes the result to the iOS power
stack.

  bsd/dev/sweet_powerinfo.c   READ ONLY probe.  It polls every 5 s, prints
                              "SWEET THERMAL: sid=N temp=..." for the five
                              PMICs and "SWEET POWER: ..." when the battery
                              numbers change, and keeps the last sample in
                              memory.  It never writes a register: the BCL
                              block is initialised by SweetBattery (0104),
                              which owns the write path.
  bsd/dev/sweet_powerinfo.h   the C interface of the probe.
  iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetPowerInfo.cpp
                              the AppleSmartBattery service.  It is an
                              IOPMPowerSource, so the iOS power stack finds
                              it the same way it finds SweetBattery, and it
                              publishes CurrentCapacity, MaxCapacity,
                              Voltage, Amperage, IsCharging, FullyCharged,
                              IsPresent, Temperature, the AppleRaw* names
                              and the XNU thermal contract ("Power Status"
                              with Thermal_Level_Warning and the CPU /
                              graphics power limits).
                              Its user client answers the
                              AppleKeyStore style selectors that the iOS
                              power stack really calls (0, 7, 14, 17, 26,
                              27, 35, 102 - the counts are in the live
                              log) and logs the others as "not supported"
                              instead of failing.

The service is created by the BSD side (bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c calls
sweet_powerinfo_init()), because the BSD layer cannot create an IOService
itself; it is attached to IOResources and registered by hand, which is
what the personality table of iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp does for the
other services of this kernel.

Boot arguments: sweetthermal=1 (the thermal probe, default off),
sweetbattery=1 (the battery probe; SweetBattery uses the same argument
for its own polling, so both services publish the same numbers),
sweetbatsid=<0..5> (the SPMI SID of the battery PMIC, default 3) and
sweetbattmah=<mAh> (the pack size, default 4600 on venus).

Two changes to bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.c are NOT part of this patch (that file
must not be touched by two patches at once); they are documented in
logs/research/impl/21-power-thermal.md:
  1. the temperature alarm blocks live in SPMI peripheral 0x0a, which is
     not in sp_peripheral_ok() yet - without that one line the alarm
     status of the PMICs stays unreadable;
  2. the ADC7 is only sampled when the PMIC itself starts a conversion:
     to drive it (EN_CTL1 0x3146, CONV_REQ 0x3147) the write allow list
     of sweet_spmi.c needs (sid 0, 0x31).

The state of charge stays a voltage derived estimate and the pack
temperature stays unavailable: both need the gauge, and the gauge is
behind the ADSP.
